#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/DFN-with-particle-size-distributions.ipynb:9
msgid "Doyle-Fuller-Newman Model with particle-size distributions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/DFN-with-particle-size-distributions.ipynb:20
msgid ""
"This notebook demonstrates the extension of the Doyle-Fuller-Newman (DFN) "
"model to include a distribution of particle sizes at every macroscale "
"location (e.g. through-cell coordinate :math:`x`) within the electrodes. "
"This model, referred to here as the Many-Particle DFN (or MP-DFN), also "
"extends the ideas of the Many-Particle Model or MPM (an :math:`x`-averaged "
"model) into the DFN framework. Note: this differs from a \"size distribution "
"in x\", where the particle size is a function of :math:`x` but with only a "
"single size at any given value of :math:`x`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/DFN-with-particle-size-distributions.ipynb:23
msgid ""
"The `MPM notebook <https://github.com/pybamm-team/PyBaMM/blob/main/docs/"
"source/examples/notebooks/models/MPM.ipynb>`__ describes how the particle-"
"size distributions are implemented in PyBaMM, and how to input parameters "
"and plot some relevant output variables. The model equations for the MP-DFN, "
"which allow for variations in the through-cell :math:`x` direction and "
"therefore include electrolyte dynamics, are similar to those of the MPM and "
"DFN, and are available in `[5] <#References>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/DFN-with-particle-size-distributions.ipynb:25
msgid ""
"By default per electrode, the DFN has 1 microscale dimension (the radial "
"coordinate within the active particles, :math:`r_{\\mathrm{k}}`, :math:"
"`\\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{n,p}`) and 1 macroscale dimension (the through-cell "
"coordinate :math:`x`), and is commonly called \"pseudo-2D\". The MP-DFN adds "
"another microscale variation with particle size :math:`R_{\\mathrm{k}}`, :"
"math:`\\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{n,p}` over an interval :math:`[R_{\\mathrm{k,"
"min}}, R_{\\mathrm{k,max}}]`, :math:`\\mathrm{k}=\\mathrm{n,p}`, and thus "
"can be thought of as \"pseudo-3D\". See `[5] <#References>`__ for more "
"details."
msgstr ""

